{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"55b8b400250ec386","name":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/WorldAtWar/units/card_unit_kyushu_j7w3","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"KYUSHU J7W3","text":"Has +2 attack and Fury if undamaged.","flavorText":"The Kyushu J7W Shinden was an experimental Japanese interceptor with an unusual canard layout and a pusher propeller. Designed in 1943 by Lieutenant Commander Masayoshi Tsuruno of the Imperial Japanese Navy, it aimed to counter B-29 raids over Japan. Flight tests of earlier canard-equipped gliders convinced the Navy to order a full prototype from Kyushu Aircraft.\r\nPowered by a 2,130 hp Mitsubishi MK9D radial engine, the Shinden carried four nose-mounted 30 mm Type 5 cannons. This gave it formidable firepower for intercepting heavy bombers. Its layout promised high speed and agility, and a jet-powered variant was envisioned for the future. Construction delays and engine cooling problems meant the first prototype only flew on 3 August 1945. Tsuruno himself piloted the short test flights, which revealed torque and vibration issues. Plans called for over a thousand units to be produced by 1947, but Japan’s surrender ended the project. Only two prototypes were built. One was scrapped and the other shipped to the US, where it remains on display at the Smithsonian’s Udvar-Hazy Center.","faction":"Japan","type":"fighter","rarity":"Unique","cost":4,"operationCost":2,"attack":3,"defense":4},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3","title":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3_title","SourceString":"KYUSHU J7W3","LocalizedString":"KYUSHU J7W3"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":3,"defense":4,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Japan","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3_text","SourceString":"Has +2 attack and Fury if undamaged.","LocalizedString":"Has +2 attack and Fury if undamaged."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3_history","SourceString":"The Kyushu J7W Shinden was an experimental Japanese interceptor with an unusual canard layout and a pusher propeller. Designed in 1943 by Lieutenant Commander Masayoshi Tsuruno of the Imperial Japanese Navy, it aimed to counter B-29 raids over Japan. Flight tests of earlier canard-equipped gliders convinced the Navy to order a full prototype from Kyushu Aircraft.\r\nPowered by a 2,130 hp Mitsubishi MK9D radial engine, the Shinden carried four nose-mounted 30 mm Type 5 cannons. This gave it formidable firepower for intercepting heavy bombers. Its layout promised high speed and agility, and a jet-powered variant was envisioned for the future. Construction delays and engine cooling problems meant the first prototype only flew on 3 August 1945. Tsuruno himself piloted the short test flights, which revealed torque and vibration issues. Plans called for over a thousand units to be produced by 1947, but Japan’s surrender ended the project. Only two prototypes were built. One was scrapped and the other shipped to the US, where it remains on display at the Smithsonian’s Udvar-Hazy Center.","LocalizedString":"The Kyushu J7W Shinden was an experimental Japanese interceptor with an unusual canard layout and a pusher propeller. Designed in 1943 by Lieutenant Commander Masayoshi Tsuruno of the Imperial Japanese Navy, it aimed to counter B-29 raids over Japan. Flight tests of earlier canard-equipped gliders convinced the Navy to order a full prototype from Kyushu Aircraft.\r\nPowered by a 2,130 hp Mitsubishi MK9D radial engine, the Shinden carried four nose-mounted 30 mm Type 5 cannons. This gave it formidable firepower for intercepting heavy bombers. Its layout promised high speed and agility, and a jet-powered variant was envisioned for the future. Construction delays and engine cooling problems meant the first prototype only flew on 3 August 1945. Tsuruno himself piloted the short test flights, which revealed torque and vibration issues. Plans called for over a thousand units to be produced by 1947, but Japan’s surrender ended the project. Only two prototypes were built. One was scrapped and the other shipped to the US, where it remains on display at the Smithsonian’s Udvar-Hazy Center."},"operationCost":2,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Passive","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardDealDamage","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand"],"extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Fury"],"hasAmbush":true,"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Japan/World_at_War/T_kyushu_j7w3.T_kyushu_j7w3","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Roy Grinnell","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/KYUSHU_J7W3.KYUSHU_J7W3","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":261.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3_photo","SourceString":"Kyushu J7W1 Shinden in July 1945.","LocalizedString":"Kyushu J7W1 Shinden in July 1945."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"ParticleSystem'P_ExplosionAircraftL'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Explosions/Large/Aircraft/P_ExplosionAircraftL.201"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeHeavy_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeHeavy.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackFighter.BP_AttackFighter_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"CustomOnDealDamageToSelf","owner":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3_C","params":[],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_BlueprintCallable | FUNC_BlueprintEvent","locals":[],"body":[{"op":"LocalFinalFunction","at":0,"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_kyushu_j7w3","signature":"card_unit_kyushu_j7w3_C.ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_kyushu_j7w3","args":[{"op":"IntConst","value":10}]},{"op":"Return","at":15,"value":{"op":